<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Calculating probabilities of non-mutually exclusive events in a sports day</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>At a sports day for <b>{@school@}</b>, there are exactly <b>{@N@} learners</b> in Form 3. The games master records the following participation data:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>{@R@}</b> learners entered running events (\(R\)).</li>
  <li><b>{@F@}</b> learners entered field events (\(F\)).</li>
  <li><b>{@B@}</b> learners entered <b>both</b> running and field events.</li>
</ul>

<p><b>a) </b> If one learner is selected at random, find the probability that they entered <b>both</b> running and field events.</p>
<p><i>(Enter your answer as a fully simplified fraction).</i></p>
<p>\(P(R \text{ and } F) =\) [[input:ans1]]</p>
<div>[[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div>

<p><b>b) </b> Because the events \(R\) and \(F\) can happen at the same time, they are <b>not mutually exclusive</b>. Find the probability that the selected learner entered <b>running events OR field events</b>.</p>
<p>\(P(R \text{ or } F) =\) [[input:ans2]]</p>
<div>[[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>

<p><b>c) </b> Let \(E_1\) be the event "entered running events ONLY" and \(E_2\) be the event "entered field events ONLY". Because a learner cannot be in the "only running" group and the "only field" group simultaneously, \(E_1\) and \(E_2\) are mutually exclusive.</p>
<p>Find the probability that the learner entered <b>exactly one</b> type of event (\(E_1 \text{ or } E_2\)).</p>
<p>\(P(E_1 \text{ or } E_2) =\) [[input:ans3]]</p>
<div>[[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>Worked Solution:</b></p>

<p><b>Part a: Joint Probability</b><br>
There are \({@B@}\) learners who entered both events, out of a total of \({@N@}\) learners.</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(R \text{ and } F) = \frac{{@B@}}{{@N@}} \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\( \displaystyle \boxed{ P(R \text{ and } F) = {@ta1@} } \)</p>

<p><b>Part b: General Addition Rule</b><br>
To find the probability of \(R \text{ or } F\), we must add the individual probabilities and subtract the intersection so we do not count the learners who did both twice:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(R \text{ or } F) = P(R) + P(F) - P(R \text{ and } F) \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(R \text{ or } F) = \frac{{@R@}}{{@N@}} + \frac{{@F@}}{{@N@}} - \frac{{@B@}}{{@N@}} \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(R \text{ or } F) = \frac{{@R + F - B@}}{{@N@}} \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\( \displaystyle \boxed{ P(R \text{ or } F) = {@ta2@} } \)</p>

<p><b>Part c: Mutually Exclusive Sub-Events</b><br>
First, we find the number of learners in each exclusive group:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Running ONLY = Total Running - Both = \({@R@} - {@B@} = {@R_only@}\)</li>
  <li>Field ONLY = Total Field - Both = \({@F@} - {@B@} = {@F_only@}\)</li>
</ul>
<p>Because these are mutually exclusive, we can simply add their probabilities together:</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(E_1 \text{ or } E_2) = P(E_1) + P(E_2) \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px;">\( \displaystyle P(E_1 \text{ or } E_2) = \frac{{@R_only@}}{{@N@}} + \frac{{@F_only@}}{{@N@}} = \frac{{@R_only + F_only@}}{{@N@}} \)</p>
<p style="margin-left: 20px; font-size: 1.2em;">\( \displaystyle \boxed{ P(E_1 \text{ or } E_2) = {@ta3@} } \)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
